at Montclair book centre rows of every  book imaginable from the new title just  published to the dusty and obscure old  book if you look hard enough you'll  probably find it here what you won't  find is customers browsing the aisles in  March  all non-essential businesses were closed  because of coronavirus the bookstore  staff faced hard decisions closed down  after 35 years in business or find a way  to keep selling nobody wanted to close  we just had to figure out the best way  to keep everybody safe and healthy while  also continuing to serve they ramped up  taking orders by phone and also online  sales and business has been so good  nobody has been laid off everybody in  fact is busy in a normal week when it  was when we were open we'd maybe have 10  internet or 10 online orders a day but  now we come in and we have at least 50  if we have anything under the under 50  that's it that's a light day for us at  this point by the front door stacks of  packages of books waiting to be shipped  out to customers a small sign of a  success story in an economy where there  have been few when many people think  about business in America they think of  big companies such as General Motors  Apple or maybe even Walmart but truth be  told more than 95% of Americans work at  small businesses like this defined as  those with less than 500 employees it's  small business that is the backbone of  the American economy that's small  business that has been decimated by  coronavirus Montclair bookstore sits in  the middle of two other stores going  through much harder times on one side a  hair salon that remains closed on the  other side a gift shop also shuttered  but in between one store that has made  it writing its own story of success  Gabriel Sandow al Jazeera Montclair New  Jersey
 

